 ## Operating characteristics
-- **Time Complexity**: $O(N_v \cdot N_c \cdot B)$, where $N_v$ is vertex count, $N_c$ is camera count, and $B$ is batch size. Depth sampling is $O(1)$ per vertex per camera.
-- **Memory Complexity**: $O(N_v + N_f + N_c \cdot H \cdot W)$ for storing masks and depth maps.
-- **Performance**: Effective for unbounded reconstructions where topological culling fails. Requires pre-computed depth maps and camera poses [S1, unofficial].
-- **Strengths**: Removes occluded geometry and background artifacts. Robust to non-manifold meshes.
-- **Limitations**: Requires accurate camera poses and depth maps. Computationally expensive for large $N_c$. Does not fix topological errors [S1, unofficial].
-- **When to Use**: Cleaning meshes from Gaussian Splatting or unbounded TSDF fusion. When topological culling is insufficient [S1, unofficial].
-- **When Not to Use**: Real-time rendering where pre-processing is not feasible. When camera poses are inaccurate [S1, unofficial].
+*   **Time Complexity**: $O(K \cdot N)$ for projection and depth sampling, where $K$ is the number of cameras and $N$ is the number of vertices. Face culling is $O(M)$ where $M$ is the number of faces.
+*   **Memory Complexity**: $O(N + M + K \cdot H \cdot W)$ for storing masks and depth maps.
+*   **Strengths**: Effective for unbounded meshes and complex geometry where local voxel occupancy is unavailable. Robust to noise by requiring multi-view consistency.
+*   **Limitations**: Requires pre-computed depth maps or rendering capability. Performance scales with the number of cameras and resolution. May cull valid geometry if depth maps are inaccurate or if the mesh is partially occluded in all views.
+*   **When to use**: When culling unbounded meshes from Gaussian Splatting or other implicit representations where voxel-based culling is not applicable.
+*   **When not to use**: For simple voxel grids where [Face Culling](8735-face-culling.md) is more efficient and accurate.
 
 ## Background
-Originated in the context of 2D Gaussian Splatting for geometrically accurate radiance fields, specifically for unbounded mesh extraction [S1, unofficial]. The method was developed to address artifacts in meshes derived from Gaussian Splatting, where traditional voxel-based culling is not applicable [S1, unofficial]. It is used in the official implementation of 2D Gaussian Splatting for geometry reconstruction on datasets like DTU and TnT [S1, unofficial].
+This method originates from the "2D Gaussian Splatting for Geometrically Accurate Radiance Fields" paper by Huang et al. (SIGGRAPH 2024) [S1, unofficial]. It was developed to address the challenge of extracting clean, geometrically accurate meshes from unbounded Gaussian Splatting reconstructions, which often contain artifacts and noise. The method leverages multi-view depth consistency to identify and remove unseen geometry, improving the quality of the extracted mesh for downstream applications.
 
